{"step_index":1957,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:45:00Z","input_tokens":3529,"cache_read_tokens":253950,"output_tokens":1429,"content":"Here is the exact truth about **Cozo**—and why it has a unique dual nature in this architecture:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical Constraint: Cozo is Embedded (RocksDB Lock)\nUnlike Qdrant or EMQX (which are client-server network daemons), **Cozo is an embedded engine**:\n* It runs in-process via `cozorocks` directly inside the process memory.\n* RocksDB places an exclusive OS lock (`LOCK` file) on its data directory.\n* **Two separate processes/containers cannot open the same Cozo directory concurrently in write mode.**\n\nBecause of that physical fact, Cozo **cannot** be a single monolithic database that every container mounts directly from disk.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What CAN Be Shared Organically vs. What Must Be Private\n\nCozo naturally splits into two distinct operational scopes:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│             1. SHARED COZO (Public Graph Substrate)         │\n│  Hosted by op-grpc-bridge / D-Bus Plugin (:8090)            │\n│                                                             │\n│  • System Graph & Crate Dependencies (`system_graph`)       │\n│  • NIST 800-53 / OSCAL Compliance Graph                    │\n│  • UI Component Gallery (`ui-gallery.db`)                  │\n│  • Public Model Registry (`model-registry.db`)              │\n│                                                             │\n│  Queryable by ANY instance over D-Bus / gRPC (Read-Only)    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                               ▲\n                               │ Queried over door\n                               │\n┌──────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┐\n│            2. PRIVATE COZO (Instance Sovereign Store)       │\n│  Stored on the instance's private ext4 slice                │\n│                                                             │\n│  • Chatbot Episodes & Conversation Turns (`chat-responses`) │\n│  • Identity Sled & Session State (`identity-cozo`)          │\n│  • Agent Episodic & Working Memory (`cognitive-memory`)     │\n│                                                             │\n│  Opened directly in-process by the container (Private Lock) │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. How This Solves \"The In-Between\"\n\n1. **Shared Knowledge Queries (Zero Disk Waste)**:\n   * When an instance needs compliance rules, OSCAL controls, or system graph metadata, it doesn't need its own copy of the database.\n   * It calls `PluginService/CallMethod` on `cozo` or `oscal_subid_registry` over the `:8090` door. The host serves the query from the central read-only catalog.\n\n2. **Private Identity Durability (Absolute Isolation)**:\n   * For the instance's own private chat turns, memories, and WireGuard session keys, it writes to its own Cozo instance residing on its dedicated ext4 partition.\n   * No locks fight with other instances, no PII is exposed to neighbors, and if an instance is destroyed, its private database is completely gone with it.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n* **Stateless Daemons (EMQX, Qdrant)** $\\rightarrow$ **Shared globally**, partitioned by topic/payload metadata.\n* **Public Cozo Graph (OSCAL, Schemas, UI Gallery)** $\\rightarrow$ **Shared centrally**, accessed as a service over the door.\n* **Personal Cozo Store (Chat, Sled, Memory)** $\\rightarrow$ **Sovereign per instance**, living on its private ext4 slice.","thinking":"Focusing on Cozo, it seems the user is questioning its shared nature. The previous discussion highlighted it as sovereign, specifically the graph store on private ext4 with a Btrfs leaf sprout. Exploring if \"organically shared\" applies to Cozo's design is the next step.\n\n\nCozo's shared nature hinges on its architecture. Public knowledge graphs and catalogs can be shared across instances because they are read-only and globally authoritative. Private data, like chat logs and identity sessions, are sovereign and cannot be shared due to sensitive information.\n\n\n"}
