{"step_index":1963,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:45:52Z","input_tokens":230479,"cache_read_tokens":28418,"output_tokens":1184,"content":"A **hub-and-spoke (spoke-and-wheel) architecture** is the ideal solution to the embedded database dilemma:\n\n---\n\n### The Hub-and-Spoke Architecture\n\n```\n                       ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                       │           THE HUB (CENTRAL ENGINE)     │\n                       │   op-grpc-bridge / Cozo Engine Core    │\n                       │                                        │\n                       │   • Datalog Query Planner & Parser     │\n                       │   • Shared In-Memory Engine            │\n                       │   • RocksDB Storage Multi-Store Router │\n                       └───────────────────┬────────────────────┘\n                                           │\n                 ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐\n                 │                         │                         │\n                 ▼                         ▼                         ▼\n        [SPOKE 1: Root Chatbot]    [SPOKE 2: User Instance]    [SPOKE 3: Hackathon Repo]\n         Data Space (ext4)          Data Space (ext4)          Data Space (ext4)\n         /var/lib/.../chatbot.img   /var/lib/.../id-1.img      /var/lib/.../hackathon.img\n         • Private Sled             • Private Sled             • Private Sled\n         • Episode Memory           • Episode Memory           • Episode Memory\n```\n\n### Why This Is Superior:\n\n1. **The Hub (Core Engine)**:\n   * Compiles and runs the heavy C++ RocksDB and Datalog execution engine **once** on the host.\n   * You don't duplicate 200 MB of database runtime memory per container.\n   * Avoids all RocksDB `LOCK` file collisions because the single hub engine manages the dispatch to each data store.\n2. **The Spokes (Data Spaces)**:\n   * Each instance gets its own physical directory or pre-formatted ext4 image on disk.\n   * Total data sovereignty: an instance's physical data stays strictly in its own spoke.\n   * If an instance is wiped or exported, only its spoke is detached or deleted.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Outline\n\nIf we establish this Hub-and-Spoke model:\n\n1. **Step 1: Multi-Store Hub Dispatch**  \n   Configure the bridge's `CozoGraphShuttle` / `DbInstance` router to maintain a map of open data spaces keyed by `session_id` / `instance_name`.\n2. **Step 2: Caller-Identity Routing**  \n   When a query arrives over `:8090` or D-Bus:\n   * The interceptor verifies the caller's WireGuard public key / session.\n   * The hub engine dispatches the Datalog query strictly against that caller's spoke data space.\n3. **Step 3: Verification**  \n   Test running parallel queries from two different identity keys and verify they read and write to completely separate spoke directories through the same engine.\n\n---\n\n**Do you confirm this Hub-and-Spoke design as our architectural path forward?**","thinking":"Confirming the user's intent to implement a spoke and wheel setup. To ensure clarity, the proposed actions will be explicitly detailed, and user confirmation sought before proceeding due to the potentially impactful nature of the changes. The goal is to accurately address the user's request.\n\n\n"}
