{"step_index":1966,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:46:48Z","input_tokens":3210,"cache_read_tokens":256887,"output_tokens":666,"content":"The **OSCAL taxonomy plugin** already provides the exact semantic routing framework needed for this hub-and-spoke model.\n\nInstead of writing custom dispatch plumbing in Rust, the taxonomy governs how the central engine routes to each spoke:\n\n---\n\n### How the OSCAL Taxonomy Resolves the Hub-and-Spoke Routing\n\nIn our sealed schema architecture, every method is tagged by its OSCAL subid:\n\n```\n[Method Call: memory_store / memory_query]\n                    │\n                    ▼\n       [OSCAL Taxonomy Classifier]\n                    │\n   ┌────────────────┴────────────────┐\n   ▼                                 ▼\n`obs.system.*` / `src.*`          `mut.service.cognitive.memory.*`\n(Public Graph & Catalog)         (Private Identity Memory)\n   │                                 │\n   ▼                                 ▼\n[The Central Hub Store]           [The Caller's Spoke Store]\n(system_graph, oscal_registry)   (Resolved via caller's SID1 / WireGuard Key)\n/var/lib/op-dbus/system.db       /var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/devices/<spoke>.img\n```\n\n1. **Category Intent (`obs.*` vs. `mut.*`)**:\n   * **`obs.system.*` and `src.*`**: Read-only public knowledge (schemas, OSCAL controls, system graph). The hub serves these from the shared central store for all callers.\n   * **`mut.service.cognitive.memory.*`**: Private state mutations. The taxonomy tags these as tenant-isolated, directing the hub engine to open and execute strictly within the caller's specific spoke directory on ext4.\n2. **Inherited by Default**:\n   * Because `ensure_category_metadata_fields` injects the taxonomy across all plugins, **every plugin method inherits this spoke-resolution behavior automatically**.\n   * Adding a new plugin or new spoke requires zero routing code—the OSCAL subid tells the engine whether an operation is public-hub or private-spoke.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Plan to Validate this Taxonomy Routing\n\n1. **Step 1: Check OSCAL Subid Tagging on Memory Methods**  \n   Confirm that `memory_store`, `memory_retrieve`, and `memory_query` in `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/cognitive.rs` carry their standard taxonomy tags (`mut.service.cognitive.memory.*` vs `obs.service.cognitive.memory.*`).\n2. **Step 2: Connect Spoke Dispatch to Caller Identity**  \n   Ensure the hub's D-Bus/gRPC router dispatches `mut.*` memory calls to the caller's spoke database path derived from their authenticated WireGuard key.\n3. **Step 3: End-to-End Test**  \n   Run a memory query from two distinct identities to confirm that public system queries hit the hub, while private memory mutations route to their respective ext4 spokes.\n\nWhenever you're ready, I can inspect the taxonomy tags and verify this routing live."}
