{"step_index":1984,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:52:00Z","input_tokens":2114,"cache_read_tokens":36676,"output_tokens":1880,"content":"Yes, exactly. Using **local sub-IDs (or sub-indices)** is the industry-standard way to manage multi-tenant shared memory without duplicating infrastructure.\n\nInstead of running separate databases, **everything lives in the shared engine, and local sub-IDs dictate what data is included or excluded during read, write, and recall.**\n\n---\n\n### How Local Sub-IDs Control Inclusion vs. Exclusion\n\nEvery memory vector in Qdrant and relation in Cozo carries two identity tags:\n1. `sub_id`: The local subsidiary/business identifier (e.g. `sub_escrow`, `sub_acquisitions`, `sub_property_ops`).\n2. `scope`: The visibility level (`conglomerate` vs `private`).\n\n```\n                              SHARED QDRANT / COZO ENGINE\n      ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n      │  Memory Record                 sub_id              scope               │\n      │  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │\n      │  \"Negotiation tactics\"         sub_acquisitions    conglomerate  ─────┐│\n      │  \"Underwriting formula v2\"     sub_acquisitions    conglomerate  ─────┤│\n      │  \"Client Deal #491 terms\"      sub_acquisitions    private       ──┐  ││\n      │  \"State lien filing rule\"      sub_escrow          conglomerate  ─────┤│\n      │  \"Escrow Account #882 balance\" sub_escrow          private       ─┐│  ││\n      └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼┼───┘\n                                                                          ││\n               QUERY FROM SUBSIDIARY: \"sub_acquisitions\"                  ││\n               Filter: (scope == \"conglomerate\" OR sub_id == \"sub_acquisitions\")\n                                                                          ││\n               ┌───────────────────────────┐   ┌──────────────────────────┘│\n               │ INCLUDED:                 │   │ EXCLUDED:                 │\n               │ • Negotiation tactics     │   │ • Escrow Account #882     │\n               │ • Underwriting formula v2 │   │   (belonging to escrow)   │\n               │ • State lien filing rule  │   │                           │\n               │ • Client Deal #491 terms  │   │                           │\n               └───────────────────────────┘   └───────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### 1. In Qdrant: Payload Tenant Indexing\nQdrant has built-in support for tenant sub-indices. In the central collection:\n* An index is created on the payload field `sub_id`.\n* When **Subsidiary A** searches the vector space, its query includes a filter:\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"filter\": {\n      \"should\": [\n        { \"key\": \"scope\", \"match\": { \"value\": \"conglomerate\" } },\n        { \"key\": \"sub_id\", \"match\": { \"value\": \"sub_acquisitions\" } }\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n* **Result**: Subsidiary A instantly retrieves all holding-company institutional learning plus its own private memories, while all private memories from other subsidiaries are excluded at the search index level.\n\n### 2. In Cozo: Datalog Identity Scoping\nIn Cozo, relations store the `sub_id` column:\n```datalog\n?[id, content] := *memories[id, sub_id, scope, content],\n                  (scope == 'conglomerate' or sub_id == $caller_sub_id)\n```\nThe query planner pushes the predicate down to the RocksDB seek operation, ensuring cross-tenant data is never loaded into memory.\n\n### 3. Outbound Control: The Local Subsidiary Decides What It Shares\nThe local business controls what enters the shared institutional pool:\n* **Promote to Conglomerate**: If an agent discovers a universal improvement (e.g. better prompt strategy, compliance guideline, market trend), it tags it `scope: \"conglomerate\"`.\n* **Keep Local**: If an agent is handling confidential customer information or sensitive financial terms, it tags it `scope: \"private\"`.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Benefits\n* **Single Engine**: You only maintain one Qdrant cluster and one Cozo engine.\n* **Instant Cross-Pollination**: Any conglomerate-scoped insight is immediately available to all businesses.\n* **Strict Privacy Boundary**: Private business data is excluded deterministically by index filters.","thinking":"The phrase likely refers to local sub-identifiers (subids), sub-indices, or sub-identities, and their potential to govern data inclusion or exclusion. Several interpretations exist due to the typos, but the core concept revolves around control over data processing based on local identifiers. The focus is on how \"local\" elements have authority over data.\n\n\nSub-identifiers and sub-indices are equivalent mechanisms for controlling data access, acting as filters that determine data inclusion or exclusion. In both vector stores and Datalog, these \"local\" identifiers govern which data is accessible based on subsidiary-specific criteria or universal visibility. Data is categorized for access based on scope, subsidiary ID, and visibility flags, ensuring data segregation between subsidiaries.\n\n\n"}
