{"step_index":1987,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:52:24Z","input_tokens":2300,"cache_read_tokens":38310,"output_tokens":1569,"content":"### Architectural Plan: Partitioning and Filtering by Subsidiary\n\nStructuring the memory and observability controls **by subsidiary** establishes a clean, multi-tenant hierarchy across the entire stack.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Subsidiary Identity Matrix\n\nEach business in the conglomerate receives a canonical `subsidiary_id` (e.g. `sub_acquisitions`, `sub_escrow`, `sub_property_ops`). This ID binds across every layer:\n\n| Layer | Shared or Subsidiary-Isolated | How `subsidiary_id` Controls It |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Qdrant (Vectors)** | **Shared Cluster** | Payload field `subsidiary_id` indexed for fast filtering. Queries filter `(subsidiary_id == $ID OR scope == \"conglomerate\")`. |\n| **Cozo (Graph)** | **Shared Engine** | Datalog relations include `subsidiary_id`. Queries isolate records by caller identity. |\n| **EMQX (Messages)** | **Shared Broker** | Topic namespace `org/conglomerate/{subsidiary_id}/#` enforced by MQTT ACLs. |\n| **Block Storage** | **Isolated per Subsidiary** | Sparse device `/var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/devices/{subsidiary_id}.img` (p1: Btrfs leaf, p2: ext4). |\n| **UI / Dashboard** | **Site-Specific** | Dedicated Svelte mount `/usr/local/share/op-dbus/dashboard/{subsidiary_id}/` or custom domain. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Data Inclusion and Exclusion Rules by Subsidiary\n\nWhen a subsidiary agent queries memory, the inclusion/exclusion filter executes at the search engine level:\n\n```\n                            INCOMING QUERY FROM: sub_escrow\n                                          │\n                  ┌───────────────────────┴───────────────────────┐\n                  ▼                                               ▼\n         [ INCLUSION CRITERIA ]                         [ EXCLUSION CRITERIA ]\n   • Any record where scope == \"conglomerate\"     • Any record where subsidiary_id != \"sub_escrow\"\n     (HQ philosophy, general laws, templates)       AND scope != \"conglomerate\"\n   • Any record where subsidiary_id == \"sub_escrow\" (Other subsidiaries' internal records, deals,\n     (Escrow's own private deal ledgers, notes)     client data, or private working memory)\n```\n\n#### Selective Peering (Inter-Subsidiary Sharing)\nIf two subsidiaries need to collaborate (e.g. *Acquisitions* handing a vetted deal over to *Escrow*):\n* The record includes `shared_with: [\"sub_escrow\"]`.\n* The inclusion filter expands to:\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"should\": [\n      { \"key\": \"scope\", \"match\": { \"value\": \"conglomerate\" } },\n      { \"key\": \"subsidiary_id\", \"match\": { \"value\": \"sub_escrow\" } },\n      { \"key\": \"shared_with\", \"match\": { \"value\": \"sub_escrow\" } }\n    ]\n  }\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Step-by-Step Implementation Plan\n\nTo wire this into our existing cognitive architecture without disrupting active services:\n\n1. **Step 1: Inspect Live Qdrant Schema & Payload Indexes**\n   * Check existing collection schemas on `127.0.0.1:6333` (`snowball_footprints`, `code_chunks_gemini`) to verify whether payload indexes already exist.\n   * Verify how metadata/payload fields are stored.\n\n2. **Step 2: Inspect Cognitive Query Builder in Rust**\n   * Review `crates/op-cognitive/src/qdrant_shuttle.rs` and `memory_store.rs` to see where Qdrant search filters are constructed.\n   * Plan how `subsidiary_id` and `scope` are passed from the caller's session identity (SID1/OIA1 token) into the query filter.\n\n3. **Step 3: Verification with a Read-Only Probe**\n   * Execute a test query against Qdrant demonstrating that a search with `subsidiary_id` filter strictly includes matching records and excludes mismatched ones.\n\n---\n\nShall I begin with **Step 1** (checking the live Qdrant collections and payload schemas)?","thinking":"The user's input clarifies that sub-ID/sub-index partitioning, along with inclusion/exclusion controls, needs organization based on subsidiary. This confirms a key structural requirement. The handler's guidance indicates a need for careful planning and direct communication before making any changes.\n\n\nDefining a subsidiary identity model is crucial for partitioning. This identity links to local storage, indexing, messaging, and web UI components. The inclusion/exclusion control matrix requires considering global, private, and inter-subsidiary shared data tiers. The implementation involves reviewing current collection schemas and configuring tenant filters for the subsidiary ID within the query builder.\n\n\n"}
